  - `--rp 1.0` filter added because a bare `--variants v3_learned_lambda` also matches the
    rp=1.2 dirs; verified it selects the 10 rp=1.0 runs and excludes the 7 rp=1.2 ones.
Default behaviour (no --latent_off) is byte-identical to before: filename, gate_mode, base loop
and log strings all unchanged. Verified schema against a real eval_results.json
(metrics.accuracy, results[].correct, metrics.gate_mode). Evals require GPU → run on cluster.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 2 RESULT] latent-off evaluation

Ran cluster/run_latent_off_eval.slurm (new; needed because the login node lacks HF_HOME +
squid proxy — a bare run fails at model load with "Connection reset by peer"). 10 paired runs.

SANITY (verification #1): all 3 v1 seeds Δ=+0.00 with identical_correctness_vector=True
(bit-identical correctness vectors) — the mode provably changes nothing when the gate is
already disabled.

RESULT (normal -> latent-off, 1.5B rp=1.0, n=1319):
  v3 learned Λ : 71.65->71.80 (+0.15), 68.69->69.67 (+0.99), 71.11->71.72 (+0.61)
                 mean 70.48 -> 71.06  (Δ +0.58, all three seeds POSITIVE)
  v4 full gate : 67.40->69.22 (+1.82), 70.81->69.52 (-1.29), 71.95->71.80 (-0.15)
                 mean 70.05 -> 70.18  (Δ +0.13, signs mixed)   [full-LoRA seeds only]
  v4 attn-LoRA : 67.70->66.94 (-0.76)  [single seed, different LoRA config — NOT pooled]

INTERPRETATION: switching the latent channel off at inference costs nothing (v3 slightly up,
v4 flat; both within seed noise). The trained models carry NO functional dependence on the
latent pathway — a CAUSAL/interventional result, strictly stronger than the prior correlational
collapse evidence (constant gate activation, Λ CV ≈ init, tied accuracies). This retires the
"Mechanism is observational" threat-to-validity in RESULTS_SECTION §6.9.

NUANCE: identical_correctness_vector=False for every v3/v4 run — individual answers flip in
both directions, so the latent is INFORMATIONALLY inert, not numerically inert. Flip counts not
yet quantified (would need the per-checkpoint eval_results_latent_off.json, which the results/
sync does not pull).

NOTE: write_latent_off_summary's per-arm grouping pooled -loraattention into the v4 mean (n=4);
the correct full-LoRA v4 mean is 70.05 -> 70.18. Grouping is cosmetic (raw rows are correct).

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 3] C1 constant-vector control (v7_constant_vector)

TWO DESIGN CORRECTIONS to the brief (both load-bearing):

(a) The brief says to substitute at "the point where e_pred is formed" in
    modeling_qwen2.py. The Eq.3 projection is NOT there — it is in
    transformers/generation/utils.py:3369-3372. More importantly, substituting in the
    GENERATION loop would leave u with ZERO gradient forever: generate() runs under
    no_grad, and the recorded thinking_embeds are detached constants by the time the
    loss forward runs, so brief check 3 would fail by construction. Instead the
    substitution is made in `thinking_residual` itself, which covers BOTH call sites
    (generation llama.py:944 and the live-graph log-prob forward llama.py:666) and puts
    u in the loss graph. An initial edit to generation/utils.py was made and then fully
    REVERTED — that file is byte-identical to HEAD (verified with git diff --quiet).

(b) u is registered CONDITIONALLY (variant_common.register_constant_latent_vector),
    not in ThinkingResidualLambda.__init__. Adding it to __init__ would change the
    state dict of every model and risk breaking load_adapter on all existing settled
    checkpoints. v1-v6 state dicts stay byte-identical. It is registered BEFORE
    get_peft_model, because modules_to_save deep-copies the Lambda module there and u
    must be inside that copy to reach the adapter.

DESIGN DECISION: the constant path deliberately SKIPS the /||probs|| normalisation.
Substituting before the divide would rescale u by a per-token varying scalar, so it
would not be a constant at all.

FREE PROPERTIES from placing u on thinking_residual_Lambda: its parameter name contains
"thinking_residual_Lambda", so patch.py puts it in Lambda's 1e-3 optimizer group; and
that module is already in GATE_MODULES/modules_to_save, so u is saved with the adapter.

Changes: variant_common — `--latent_source {projection,constant}` (default projection, so
every existing variant is unchanged), _constant_latent_thinking_residual,
register_constant_latent_vector, apply_constant_latent_patch (raises if u not registered),
v7_constant_vector appended AFTER v6 in VARIANT_CHAIN (indices unshifted),
GATE_MODES["v7_constant_vector"]="unit", latent_source added to _CONFIG_FINGERPRINT so a
resume cannot silently mix sources. plot_style — v7 label + colour. New
hrpo_constant_vector.py (v3 copy; --latent_source projection takes v3's exact code path).
parse_experiment_name verified to parse 1.5b-v7_constant_vector[-seedN][-rp1].

NOTE: the brief's checklist assumes an "existing CPU test suite" — there is none (no
tests/, no test files, no pytest config; pytest is in requirements). Created tests/ with
tests/test_constant_vector.py.

CHECKS: not yet run — torch is unavailable on the local box, so the pytest file must run
on the cluster. The algebra behind check 2 was confirmed independently in pure python
(substituting u for the residual is exact when u == residual).

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 3b] u-init scale diagnostic + rescale options

Added --constant_init {mean,mean_scaled,random_row} (default "mean" = the brief's spec)
and constant_init_stats(). register_constant_latent_vector now PRINTS ||u||, mean||e_i||
and the ratio, and warns when u starts >10x smaller than a typical embedding row.
Rationale: for a peaked probs the real Eq.3 output is close to a single embedding ROW,
but the embedding CENTROID has a far smaller norm (rows cancel) — so the brief's "mean"
init is on-manifold in direction but likely NOT scale-matched, and u would have to climb
from near-zero at lr 1e-3. "mean_scaled" keeps the centroid direction at row scale;
"random_row" is on-manifold in both senses. constant_init added to _CONFIG_FINGERPRINT.
Tests extended to cover the scale gap and the mean_scaled rescale.

KNOWN SHARP EDGE: experiment_dir has no suffix for latent_source, so running
hrpo_constant_vector.py with --latent_source projection (the check-1 v3 reproduction)
targets the SAME dir as constant mode. The _CONFIG_FINGERPRINT guard refuses a resume
across that change, so nothing mixes silently, but check-1 runs should use a scratch dir.
Not changed because the suffix regex only matches -(seed|rp|lora) and extending it would
touch discovery for every existing run.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 3 CHECKS PASSED + u-init decision]

pytest tests/test_constant_vector.py: 8/8 PASSED on the cluster. Covers brief check 2
(constant path bit-identical to the unit-gate path when u == residual), check 3 (u receives
a non-zero gradient — the check the brief's generation-site design would have FAILED), the
structural half of check 1 (projection branch installs v3's exact patch), Lambda's gradient
path surviving the swap, the registration-ordering guard, and the two init-scale tests.

u-INIT DECISION: keep --constant_init mean (the brief's spec, already the default).
Measured on Qwen2.5-1.5B (151936 x 1536): centroid norm 0.3270 vs mean row norm 1.0126,
ratio 0.3229 — u starts only 3.1x smaller than what it replaces. My earlier synthetic
estimate of ~63x was unrepresentative: it used i.i.d. zero-centred Gaussians, where the
centroid cancels to ~1/sqrt(V), whereas real LLM embedding tables carry a large shared
common-direction component. 3.1x is the same order of magnitude, u is 1536 params at lr
1e-3, and the blend already scales the latent by the hidden ratio (~0.167). mean_scaled and
random_row remain available if C1 diagnostics show u stalling.

BUGFIX (found while checking the above): GateGradientLogger matched
"thinking_residual_Lambda" in name, which ALSO matches the C1 vector
...thinking_residual_Lambda.u — so v7 would have logged Lambda+u pooled into one bucket and
contaminated the gradient-flow diagnostic. u now has its own bucket, matched first
(endswith ".u"). Empty/null for v1-v6, which have no u, so existing runs are unaffected.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] stage0_warmstart.py + projection extraction + --init_from

PROJECTION EXTRACTED (the Phase-1 item-3 blocker): Eq.3 is now
transformers.generation.utils.hrpo_project_latent(probs, embed_weight), called by the
_sample loop instead of being inlined there. Operation order preserved exactly (einsum,
then in-place divide by the L2 norm cast to the result dtype), and the reshape is a no-op
for the 2-D generation case, so rollouts are bit-identical. This is the ONLY change to
vendored generation code; tests/test_projection_extraction.py pins it against a verbatim
copy of the original inline expression (fp32/fp64, peaked and uniform p, and the
sequence-shaped path vs a per-position loop).

stage0_warmstart.py: one teacher-forced epoch over GSM8K-train.
  - Prompt identical to RL (utils.SYSTEM_PROMPT + question); target = reference reasoning
    (calculator <<...>> annotations stripped, since RL never rewards emitting them) plus
    "#### <ans>"; loss masked to the assistant span.
  - Think span mirrors the RL rule (is_thinking while the decoded string does not yet
    contain "####"), evaluated on the teacher-forced target. Position 0 is always False —
    its latent would come from position -1.
  - --blend on: TWO passes. Pass 1 (no_grad) -> logits -> softmax(/temperature) -> Eq.3 via
    the shared helper -> latents, shifted by one. Pass 2 passes input_ids + inputs_embeds +
    thinking_mask together, which is what routes through unsloth's blend
    (llama.py:606-607, 663-669) exactly as rl_replacements.py:410 does. Latents are
    DETACHED deliberately: in RL they come from no_grad generation and are already
    constants in the loss forward, so this is faithful, not a shortcut.
  - Projection chunked over positions (--proj_chunk): probs is (B,S,152k), whole-sequence
    materialisation would cost GBs.
  - --blend off is a genuinely PLAIN LoRA SFT: modules_to_save=None, no Lambda reset, no
    unit-gate patch, answer_start="". Two reasons: it makes check 1 ("bit-for-bit plain
    PEFT SFT") literally true, and it is required for arm C3 — v1 has modules_to_save=None
    so it cannot load an adapter carrying Lambda.
  - Output experiments/{size}-stage0-{on|off}-rmin{r}-seed{N}/ + stage0_log.jsonl (loss,
    Lambda mean, per-dim hidden-ratio CV, mean hidden ratio, Lambda grad norm, every 25
    steps). The dir name deliberately does NOT match EXPERIMENT_NAME_RE, so eval_all and
    analyse_replicates cannot mistake a warm start for an ablation result.
  - Smoke knobs --limit/--max_steps/--freeze_lambda for cheap verification.

--init_from: added to build_arg_parser and _CONFIG_FINGERPRINT, with
variant_common.load_stage0_init() doing the load. It RAISES on unexpected keys rather than
warning — a silent mismatch would leave a run cold-started while its logs claimed
otherwise — reports missing keys and max |dLambda|, and warns if Lambda did not move.
Wired into hrpo_grpo_baseline.py (C3) and hrpo_learned_lambda.py (T1), in both cases AFTER
get_peft_model (the loader writes into the PEFT adapter).

r_min: Stage 0 uses the shared --residual_r_min default of 0.98 per the human's Phase-1
decision (match the existing -rp1 arms), NOT the brief's 0.95.

CHECKS 1-4 NOT YET RUN: all four need a GPU.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] cluster/run_stage0.slurm

Batch wrapper for stage0_warmstart.py. Env block byte-identical to the working
run_eval_and_analysis.slurm (HF_HOME + squid proxy) — a bare login-node run fails at model
load, as both earlier attempts did.

Adds a PREFLIGHT enforcing the brief's ground rule 0: asserts transformers/unsloth/trl all
resolve INSIDE the repo (a pip install would silently shadow the vendored forks with stock,
gate-less packages and every run would train with no latent path), and that the extracted
hrpo_project_latent helper is importable. Fails in seconds rather than after burning GPU.

Knobs via env: MODEL_SIZE, BLEND{on,off}, SEED, R_MIN (default 0.98, matching the -rp1 arms),
BATCH_SIZE, GRAD_ACCUM, PROJ_CHUNK, LIMIT, MAX_STEPS, FREEZE_LAMBDA, plus SMOKE=1 which sets
LIMIT=64 MAX_STEPS=10 for a cheap machinery check.

BATCH_SIZE defaults to 4 (not RL's 8) with GRAD_ACCUM 8, keeping the effective batch at 32:
Stage-0 pass 1 materialises a (batch, seq, 152k) logits tensor that RL's rollout path never
holds, so the memory profile is different. If it still OOMs, lower BATCH_SIZE then PROJ_CHUNK.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] test fix: batched-vs-loop projection is FP-equal, not bit-equal

pytest run on the cluster: 12/13 passed. The one failure,
test_sequence_shape_equals_per_position_loop, was a BAD ASSERTION on my part, not a code
defect: it demanded torch.equal between one (B*S,V)@(V,D) GEMM and B separate (S,V)@(V,D)
GEMMs. BLAS does not promise the same accumulation order across different shapes, so the
last ULPs differ — observed deviations ~1e-6/1e-7 in fp32 over a 512-term reduction
(e.g. -1.6080e-04 vs -1.6096e-04, 9.1472e-02 vs 9.1473e-02).

Relaxed to allclose(rtol=1e-5, atol=1e-5) PLUS an explicit max-deviation bound of 1e-4, so a
genuine defect (wrong reshape, transposed einsum, mis-broadcast norm — all of which move
values by O(0.1-1)) still fails by 5+ orders of magnitude.

IMPORTANTLY the bitwise guarantee that protects existing arms was never in question and
PASSED: test_matches_original_bitwise (fp32 and fp64) plus the peaked/uniform test cover the
2-D generation shape, where the reshape is a no-op. Rollouts remain bit-identical.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke results: blend=off OK, blend=on hit a dtype bug (fixed)

Jobs 16047 (off), 16048 (on+freeze_lambda), 16049 (on). A100-80GB.

PREFLIGHT WORKED: "preflight OK: vendored transformers/unsloth/trl + Eq.3 helper present" —
the ground-rule-0 guard against pip-shadowed forks fires correctly.

16047 --blend off: COMPLETED. 10 steps, train_loss 4.27, wrote checkpoint-10 +
stage0_summary.json. final_lambda=None and no stage0_log.jsonl, both correct: the Lambda
diagnostics are guarded to blend_on. Confirms the plain-SFT path runs with no gate code path.
(Note: "epoch 5.0" is a smoke artifact — 64 examples at an effective batch of 32 is 2 steps
per epoch, so --max_steps 10 loops the subset 5x. Expected, not a defect.)

16048 + 16049 --blend on: BOTH FAILED, identical error, my bug:
    stage0_warmstart.py:209 _project -> hrpo_project_latent -> einsum
    RuntimeError: expected scalar type BFloat16 but found Float
_project upcast logits with .float() for a numerically stable softmax (good) but then fed
fp32 probs into the einsum against the bf16 embedding table. FIXED by casting probs back to
embed_weight.dtype before the einsum — at the CALLER, deliberately not inside
hrpo_project_latent: a silent internal cast would both mask caller bugs and change the dtype
behaviour of the rollout path the helper is a faithful extraction of. Casting back also keeps
the einsum in the same dtype generation uses, so Stage 0 stays faithful to the RL path.
Regression test added (test_mixed_dtype_probs_and_embedding_must_be_cast_by_the_caller).

Pass-1 MEMORY IS STILL UNTESTED: both blended runs died before the projection completed, so
the (batch, seq, 152k) logits cost has not actually been measured. The node is an 80GB A100,
so BATCH_SIZE=4 is likely conservative.

Also fixed: run_stage0.slurm printed a stage0_log.jsonl path for blend=off runs, which never
produce that file.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke round 2: dtype fixed, but CHECK 2b FAILED

Jobs 16083 (on+freeze_lambda) and 16084 (on) both COMPLETED — the bf16/fp32 einsum fix works,
and pass 1's (batch, seq, 152k) projection ran without OOM on the 80GB A100 at BATCH_SIZE=4.

BUT Lambda DID NOT MOVE in either run:
  initial == final == lambda_mean 6.833611488342285, hidden_ratio 0.13819901645183563,
  hidden_ratio_cv 0.30054810643196106  (bit-identical), and lambda_grad_norm = null.
Check 2a (frozen Lambda unchanged) therefore "passes" only VACUOUSLY — Lambda does not move
even when trainable, so the test cannot distinguish frozen from not. Check 2b FAILS: no
gradient ever reached Lambda, i.e. the blend is contributing nothing to the loss.

Ruled out: **kwargs IS forwarded through CausalLM_fast_forward to the inner model
(llama.py, "outputs = self.model(..., *args, **kwargs)"), so thinking_mask does propagate.

Instrumented rather than guessed — three diagnostics added for the next smoke run:
  1. think-span statistics printed after dataset build (mean/min/max think tokens per example
     and how many examples have an EMPTY span), raising if every span is empty. An empty mask
     is the leading hypothesis: the blend then never fires and Lambda gets no gradient.
  2. an assertion that Lambda is trainable and present in an optimizer param group
     (skipped under --freeze_lambda, where requires_grad=False is the intent).
  3. Stage0Logger now fires on the first few steps as well — with every=25 and a 10-step smoke
     run it only ever logged step 0, which is why the logs were nearly empty.

Also noted: both blend=on runs write to the SAME output dir (freeze_lambda is not part of the
name), so the second overwrote the first and stage0_log.jsonl holds appended records from
both. Same class of sharp edge as latent_source not being in experiment_dir.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke round 3: mask and optimizer RULED OUT, blend counter added

Job 16092 (--blend on, with the new diagnostics):
  think-span over 64 examples: mean 88.2 tokens, min 24, max 276; mean seq len 231.5;
  examples with EMPTY think span: 0/64          <- mask hypothesis DISPROVED
  trainable Lambda params: ['...thinking_residual_Lambda.modules_to_save.default.Lambda']
  Lambda tensors in optimizer: 1                <- optimizer hypothesis DISPROVED
  initial == final lambda_mean 6.833611488342285 (bit-identical), grad norm still null.

So Lambda gets no gradient even though the think mask is substantial (88 of ~231 tokens) and
Lambda is both trainable and in an optimizer param group. The remaining explanation is that
the forward never reaches the blend at all.

Added a decisive, callback-independent instrument: thinking_residual is wrapped with a
counter (BLEND_CALLS) after the unit-gate patch, and the run prints how many times the blend
executed and how many masked rows were blended, with an explicit ERROR line if the count is 0.

Separately noted (secondary): Stage0Logger is registered (verified in source) and
on_pre_optimizer_step DOES exist in transformers 4.50.3 (trainer_callback.py:387), yet run
16092 appended nothing to stage0_log.jsonl (file mtime is still from the previous jobs).
Suspect unsloth's patched _fast_inner_training_loop does not dispatch that hook. Not chased
further because the initial/final lambda_diagnostics do not depend on the callback and
already establish the finding.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke round 4: blend EXECUTES; problem is gradient flow, not routing

Job 16108: "[stage0] blend executions: 80 calls, 28230 masked rows blended"
80 = 10 optimizer steps x 8 grad-accum micro-batches, i.e. thinking_residual fires on every
forward, and ~28k positions are actually blended. So the forward DOES reach
llama.py:663-669 and thinking_mask propagates correctly.

Yet Lambda is still bit-identical (6.833611488342285) with a null grad norm. Combined with
the earlier results this rules out, with evidence, all three of: empty think mask (0/64
empty, mean 88.2 tokens), Lambda not trainable / not in optimizer (1 tensor, correct name),
and the blend not executing (80 calls).

=> The remaining failure mode is GRADIENT FLOW: Lambda is not connected to the loss graph,
or the connection is severed between thinking_residual's output and the loss. Added a
one-shot autograd probe in compute_loss that calls torch.autograd.grad(loss, Lambda,
allow_unused=True) on the first step and reports loss.requires_grad,
Lambda.requires_grad and whether the gradient is None — answering the question directly
instead of by elimination.

Leading suspect to check next: use_gradient_checkpointing="unsloth". The blend happens at the
embedding level, upstream of the checkpointed decoder layers; if unsloth's offloaded
checkpointing detaches at that boundary, parameters used BEFORE the first checkpointed
segment would receive no gradient while the LoRA parameters inside the segments train
normally — which matches the observed pattern exactly (loss decreases, Lambda frozen).

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke round 5: Lambda is NOT in the loss graph (confirmed)

Job 16368 probe:
  [stage0][probe] loss.requires_grad=True Lambda.requires_grad=True grad_is_None=True
torch.autograd.grad(loss, Lambda, allow_unused=True) returns None. So with the blend
confirmed executing (80 calls, 28230 rows), the think mask dense, and Lambda both trainable
and in the optimizer, the defect is definitively a BROKEN GRADIENT CONNECTION between the
blend output and the optimised Lambda — not routing, masking or optimiser configuration.

Leading suspect now: _unit_gate_thinking_residual computes a_t = self.thinking_residual_Lambda(ones),
and after get_peft_model that attribute is a PEFT ModulesToSaveWrapper. Its forward falls back
to original_module (the FROZEN copy) when adapters are disabled or the active adapter is not in
modules_to_save. If that fallback is taken, a_t is built from the frozen Lambda: the blend runs,
the loss is fine, and the gradient goes to a parameter nobody optimises — exactly the observed
signature. (Note v3 RL does get Lambda gradients, so whatever differs is specific to the Stage-0
HF Trainer setup rather than to the patch itself.)

Added a one-shot in-blend probe reporting embeds/residual/a_t/out requires_grad plus the Lambda
module type, its original_module, which parameters inside it are trainable, and
disable_adapters/active_adapter/modules_to_save keys.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] smoke round 6: wrapper suspect DISPROVED; checkpointing now prime

Job 16369 in-blend probe:
  embeds.rg=True residual.rg=False a_t.rg=True out.rg=True
  Lambda module=ModulesToSaveWrapper (orig=ThinkingResidualLambda)
      trainable_inside=['modules_to_save.default.Lambda']
  disable_adapters=False active=default keys=['default']
So PEFT dispatches to the TRAINABLE Lambda, a_t and the blend output both require grad — the
ModulesToSaveWrapper/original_module hypothesis is DISPROVED. The connection is intact AT the
blend; the break is downstream, between the blended embeddings and the loss.

Decisive clue: embeds.rg=True even though embed_tokens is frozen. inputs_embeds only requires
grad because something explicitly marked it — the standard enable_input_require_grads trick
that exists precisely to make GRADIENT CHECKPOINTING work with frozen embeddings. Combined
with loss.requires_grad=True (LoRA inside the segments trains, loss falls) while Lambda
upstream of the first segment gets nothing, this is the classic signature of a checkpoint
boundary that does not propagate gradient to its input.

Ruled out on inspection: the .detach() at llama.py:713 is inside a DPO-only branch
(_has_no_labels), not our path.

Two changes to test diagnosis and fix in one run:
  1. --grad_checkpoint {off,unsloth,True}, DEFAULT OFF for Stage 0 (memory is not the
     constraint — 80GB at batch 4 — correctness is). Exposed via GRAD_CKPT in run_stage0.slurm.
  2. a bisection probe: the blend output is captured and grad(loss, blend_output) reported.
     None => the break is downstream of the blend; not-None => between blend output and Lambda.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] ROOT CAUSE FOUND: unsloth detaches inputs_embeds (llama.py:707)

Job 16370 supplied the missing bisection: "grad(loss, blend_output) is None: True" — the break
is DOWNSTREAM of the blend — and turning gradient checkpointing off did NOT help, killing that
hypothesis too.

CAUSE (unsloth/models/llama.py:707, a DPO-oriented branch):
    if getattr(self, "_has_no_labels", False) is True and (attention_mask is not None) \
       and (past_key_values is None) and (not train_embed_tokens):
        if not inputs_embeds.is_leaf:
            inputs_embeds = inputs_embeds.detach()          # severs the graph
        ...
        if inputs_requires_grad: inputs_embeds.requires_grad_(True)   # fresh leaf
with llama.py:1067  self.model._has_no_labels = labels is None.

The Stage-0 call met ALL FOUR conditions: labels not passed (we compute CE ourselves),
attention_mask passed, no past_key_values, embed_tokens frozen. So immediately AFTER the Eq.4
blend the embeddings were detached and re-marked as a fresh leaf. That is why every
observation held simultaneously and looked contradictory: the blend executed (80 calls), a_t
and the blend output required grad, Lambda was trainable and in the optimizer, loss.requires_grad
was True and the loss fell (LoRA lives INSIDE the layers, downstream of the new leaf) — while
Lambda, upstream of it, received nothing.

Why v3 RL was unaffected: rl_replacements.py:409 calls the model as
    trainer.model(input_ids=..., inputs_embeds=..., thinking_mask=..., logits_to_keep=...)
with NO attention_mask, so condition 2 fails and the detach never fires. The bug was specific
to how I called the model, not to the blend, the mask, the optimizer or checkpointing — all of
which the earlier rounds had (correctly) eliminated.

FIX: stop passing attention_mask to the model in stage0_warmstart.compute_loss (both passes),
matching the RL log-prob call exactly. Safe: unsloth nulls attention_mask during training
anyway (llama.py:726-728), padding is already excluded from the loss via labels=-100 and from
the think mask, so no attention behaviour changes.

HARDENED: the one-shot probe now RAISES instead of printing if grad(loss, Lambda) is None, so a
decorative blend can never again pass silently as a warm start.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] fix CONFIRMED by the failure mode; probe replaced with a hook

Job 16371 (first run with attention_mask removed) crashed — but on the PROBE, not on training:
    RuntimeError: When use_reentrant=True, torch.utils.checkpoint is incompatible
    with .grad() or passing an `inputs` parameter to .backward()

That error is itself the confirmation the fix worked. In job 16370 (attention_mask still
passed) torch.autograd.grad(loss, Lambda, allow_unused=True) returned None IMMEDIATELY, because
Lambda was disconnected and no traversal was required. With attention_mask removed the graph now
extends back THROUGH the checkpointed decoder layers to the blend and Lambda, so autograd.grad
must traverse a reentrant checkpoint — which PyTorch forbids. The probe could only hit this
error once Lambda became reachable.

Replaced the probe with a BACKWARD HOOK on the trainable Lambda (LAMBDA_GRAD), which counts
gradient arrivals and the max |grad| during the ordinary backward. This is checkpointing-safe
(no .grad() call) and is a stronger check anyway: it proves gradient actually FLOWS to Lambda
in real training rather than that a graph path merely exists. The run now RAISES if zero
gradients ever arrive, so a decorative blend can never pass silently as a warm start.

Note gradient checkpointing was evidently still active despite --grad_checkpoint off; not
chased, because the hook is correct either way and checkpointing is desirable for the full run.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] warm starts TRAINED; checks 3 & 4 tooling

Real one-epoch warm starts completed (233 steps each, seed 42, r_min 0.98):
  blend=on  (job 16373): 1864 Lambda grad arrivals (= 233 steps x 8 accum), max |grad|
            1.689e-02; 1864 blend executions, 641,075 masked rows. Lambda 6.833611 ->
            6.764175; hidden_ratio 0.138199 -> 0.142826 (+3.3%); cv 0.30055 -> 0.29712.
            train_loss 3.785. The latent fraction RISES — a genuine warm start. For scale,
            v3 RL converges at hidden_ratio 0.1671, so Stage 0 covers ~10% of that gap
            before RL starts.
  blend=off (job 16374): train_loss 3.777, final_lambda None (correct — no Lambda at all).

Checks 1, 2a, 2b are now satisfied. Tooling added for the remaining two:
  * --init_only (build_arg_parser) makes check 3 cost seconds: the RL script loads the warm
    start, reports, and exits before training. load_stage0_init now also prints the resulting
    lambda_mean so it can be compared directly against stage0_summary.json's final value
    (blend=on: 6.7641754150390625).
  * eval_stage0.py for check 4. Stage-0 dirs are deliberately invisible to eval_all, and
    eval_gsm8k.py's __main__ infers base model/temperature from the path (a `-temp` suffix)
    which they lack, so they need their own entry point. Gate mode mirrors training
    (on -> "unit", off -> "disabled"); results go to eval_results_stage0.json, never
    eval_results.json, so nothing can leak into the ablation comparison table.
  * cluster/run_stage0_checks.slurm runs 3a, 3b and 4 in one job.

## [Stage 0 brief — Phase 4] checks 3 & 4; WARM-START ARM COLLISION found and fixed

CHECK 4 (job 16475): zero-shot GSM8K of the warm starts vs base 61.11 —
  stage0 --blend on  59.06% (-2.05)     stage0 --blend off 58.98% (-2.13)
Both slightly BELOW base, and only 0.08 apart. Expected and flagged in advance: one SFT epoch
optimises next-token likelihood, not answer correctness, and at hidden_ratio ~0.14 the blend
contributes little. The warm start's value is whether it improves the RL STARTING POINT, which
only T1 vs A1 in Phase 5 can answer — this number neither confirms nor refutes it.

CHECK 3 DID NOT RUN, and why is important: both invocations printed
  "Experiment ./experiments/1.5b-v3_learned_lambda-rp1 already has a completed checkpoint"
exit_if_complete() fires at the top of main(), long before --init_from. The cause is that
experiment_dir() did not include init_from in the suffix, so a WARM-STARTED run resolved to the
SAME directory as its cold counterpart — i.e. arm T1 mapped onto the settled A1 result. It
exited harmlessly only because that directory is complete; on an incomplete one it would have
trained into a directory holding a dissertation number.

A second, quieter hazard from the same root: analyse_replicates.parse_suffix extracted only
seed/rp/lora, so T1 and A1 would have produced the SAME arm key and been pooled as two seeds of
one arm — silently averaging a treatment with its own control.

FIX (three coordinated changes):
  * experiment_dir appends -warm{on|off} when --init_from is set (appended last, so no existing
    directory name changes);
  * EXPERIMENT_NAME_RE accepts warm in the suffix alternation;
  * analyse_replicates.parse_suffix returns a 4th element and the arm key becomes
    (size, variant, rp, lora, warm); arm_label reports "· warm-start {tag}".
Consumers updated for the new arity: generate_report._seed_arms and _primary_arms (which now
also EXCLUDES warm arms from the cold ablation cells), and all comparison keys in
analyse_replicates. Added the comparisons the brief actually wants: T1 vs A1 and C3 vs v1.
Verified end to end: A1 and T1 get distinct dirs AND distinct arm keys, while T1 seeds 42/43
still group together; every pre-existing arm still parses with warm=None, so nothing already on
disk changes meaning.

---
## D2 programme (2026-10-02): rollout parameter noise + exploration controls

Goal: test whether restoring exploration (QeRL-style RMSNorm weight noise, and plain rollout
temperature / learning-rate controls) improves GSM8K accuracy of GRPO (v1) and HRPO (v3) under
Dr. GRPO, before building anything latent-specific. Everything below is OFF by default; with the
new flags unset every pre-existing code path and directory name is unchanged.

Files added: noise_ext.py, eval_d2.py, analyse_d2.py, cluster/submit_d2.sh, cluster/run_eval_d2.slurm,
tests/test_noise_ext.py, tests/test_new_arm_naming.py.
Files changed: variant_common.py only.

variant_common.py
  * --noise_sigma_start/--noise_sigma_end/--noise_stages/--noise_mode (default: off). apply_exploration_noise()
    is called from train_with_resume next to apply_dr_grpo, so every variant script gets it.
  * experiment_dir: a run that moves the rollout temperature (!= 0.5), the learning rate (!= 5e-6) or turns
    noise on is named "new-<size>-<variant>-...-t0.8 / -lr1e-05 / -noise0.05to0.005". The "new-" prefix keeps
    EXPERIMENT_NAME_RE from matching, so eval_all / analyse_replicates / generate_report never see these
    directories. Reason: parse_suffix ignores unknown tokens, which would pool a noisy run with its own
    Dr. GRPO control as extra seeds of one arm (the same hazard as the warm-start fix above). eval_d2.py and
    analyse_d2.py own the "new-" directories. Before this change a temperature/lr probe would have resolved
    to the SAME directory as its baseline (temperature and lr were fingerprinted but not in the name).
  * Noise keys added to _CONFIG_FINGERPRINT with resume-safe defaults in _FINGERPRINT_DEFAULTS.
  * --repetition_penalty default 1.2 -> 1.0. 1.0 is the only value used for any reported result; the old
    default let a forgotten flag train an off-paper run. experiment_dir still suffixes "-rp1" for 1.0, so
    every settled directory name is unchanged.

KNOWN PROTOCOL FACT (not new, but now stated): --seed reaches only get_peft_model(random_state=...), i.e. the
LoRA/Lambda initialisation. The variant scripts build GRPOConfig without a seed, so data order and the trainer
RNG are the HF default (42) for every seed (grpo_trainer.py:364 set_seed(args.seed=42)). Same-seed baseline and
treatment share init and data order, which makes per-seed comparisons paired, but the seed SD is an
initialisation SD, not a full training-seed SD. D2 runs follow the same convention so the settled Dr. GRPO
baselines stay valid controls.

Verified locally with torch/scipy stubbed: naming/isolation logic and the analyse_d2 pipeline on synthetic
experiment directories. NOT verified: noise_ext.py (needs torch; tests/test_noise_ext.py must be run on the
cluster) and everything that touches a GPU.
